{
  "id": "ss.env.stage-pan",
  "name": "Salt Pan stage card (SS)",
  "description": "The picture on the Salt Pan card in Feelers' map select: the stage drawn once, from the camera's own height.\n\nIt is the pan's own floor \u2014 $sand lifted a stop under a flat glare wash, dune-scale swells in three concentric steps each, and wind ripples banded at the -13 degrees the pan's tile bands them at, every ripple a wide lee with the lit face inside it so it falls off instead of stopping. Light on sand is $chitin and not $sand.light: the light ramp mixes toward white, and a sand highlight built that way comes out grey where sun on sand is golden. Under the drift is the crust the stage is named for, as salt bloom and a handful of cracks rather than the hex lattice the floor tile had to give up for tiling like a grid, and the field's own rust flecks are in the sand so the two grounds read as one world.\n\nThis is the one card that has to be bright. Three stage cards sit side by side in the menu and the other two are ground with no sky over it; the pan is the one with the sun on it, and it earns its place in the row by being a stop up from them before a single prop is laid on it. What is laid on it is sparse on purpose \u2014 seven blocking props against the field's nine, because the pan's whole bargain is open ground.\n\nAuthored 3:2. The card fits it to the strip's width and crops it vertically, never horizontally, so the weight sits in a band through the middle and props run off the left and right edges, where the frame is the only thing that cuts them.",
